  [PASS] 7B THE RESIDUAL IS ALREADY THERE AT 1 keV, and it is NOT the hot gas.  Twenty X-ray groups with the gas actually measured -- 2 to 14e13 Msun, an order of magnitude below a cluster -- still need eta ~ 2 at R500 on both footings once a standard stellar mass is added.  So the framework's cluster residual is not a cluster-scale phenomenon, and 'no missing mass below sigma = 300' does not survive an X-ray sample   (canonical eta(R500) = 1.80 - 2.11 over the stellar bracket (alt 1.54 - 1.81); scatter of log eta(R500) = 0.117 dex over 20 groups; closing it needs 4.7x the SHMR stellar mass)
  [PASS] 7B-radial the residual is far LARGER at R2500 than at R500 -- it is centrally concentrated, which is the known shape of the MOND cluster discrepancy (Sanders 2003, Angus+2008) and is the OPPOSITE of what a diffuse missing-baryon component would give.  Whatever supplies it sits in the middle of the group, not in its outskirts   (canonical eta = 3.31-4.94 at R2500 (g/a_0 = 0.47) vs 1.80-2.11 at R500 (g/a_0 = 0.22))
  [PASS] 7B-trend eta DOES rise with temperature inside the group range -- but as a smooth POWER LAW, not a step.  A single power law in kT leaves under 0.1 dex of residual, so there is no threshold, no switch-on temperature, and nowhere for a second constant of nature to sit.  The residual is a continuous function of scale   (d log eta(R500)/d log kT = +0.622 over 20 groups; residual scatter about that single power law = 0.067 dex, against a total eta range of 0.38 dex -- one line explains 82% of it)
  the alternative, computed beside: NEWTONIAN dynamics needs a baryon fraction of only 0.099 of the hydrostatic mass, i.e. 10.1x more mass than the baryons.  The framework reduces the group missing mass by a factor 5.2 but does not remove it.
  [PASS] M7B-1 mutation: with nu = 1 (no modification at all) the required-mass ratio must collapse to the plain Newtonian ratio, and it does -- the kernel is doing real work here, it is not a rescaling   (nu = 1 gives eta = 10.1; the kernel gives 1.80)
  [PASS] M7B-2 mutation: tripling a_0 must REDUCE the required baryons.  In the DEEP-MOND limit g = sqrt(G M a_0)/r gives M proportional to 1/a_0 exactly, so the ratio must be 1/3, approached from above because nu is not fully saturated at g/a_0 = 0.2.  (An earlier version of this check wrote 1/sqrt(3), which is the acceleration scaling, not the mass scaling -- corrected here.)   (eta(3 a_0) = 0.68 vs eta(a_0) = 1.80, ratio 0.378 (deep-MOND limit 1/3 = 0.333))

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 7 -- verdict
====================================================================================================================
  Branch A (optical, stars only) and branch B (X-ray, stars + measured gas) do not overlap in the catalogue but
  they overlap in MASS.  The Lovisari groups carry 1.4-11e12 Msun of hot gas, the same order as the stars in the
  log L_K = 11.5-12 optical groups.  At that mass branch A (stars only) gives eta = 9.2 and
  branch B (stars + measured gas) gives eta = 1.80-2.11.  Most of that improvement is branch A's
  selection bias, not the gas: the gas is only ~1-3x the stellar mass in these systems, so counting it can move
  eta by at most a factor of a few, and the X-ray branch shows where it actually lands.
  [PASS] 7 VERDICT -- a LIABILITY, recorded against interest.  The item asked whether the group missing mass is the hot gas that optical catalogues do not count.  It is not.  With the gas measured and a standard stellar mass, 1 keV groups still need eta = 1.2-2.6 at R500 and 2.5-5 at R2500, on both footings, with no step and no temperature threshold.  There is no second constant of nature at the group scale, and the cluster residual reaches an order of magnitude further down in mass than the 'clusters only' framing allows   (canonical eta(R500) = 1.80-2.11, alt 1.54-1.81, 20 groups of 2-14e13 Msun; a passing result would have been eta = 1.0 inside the stellar bracket)
  what could still rescue it, stated so it can be tested rather than assumed: (i) hydrostatic bias runs the
  WRONG way -- HSE masses are typically 10-30% LOW, which lowers eta only slightly and cannot manufacture 2x;
  (ii) the stellar mass is the one imported quantity, and closing eta = 1 at R500 needs 4.7x the
  SHMR value, which is well outside its 0.2 dex scatter; (iii) a cold/warm baryon reservoir of the same size as
  the hot gas, centrally concentrated, would do it -- that is a prediction the item makes and X-ray data do not
  presently test.

